# Reverse proxy for a single-VM deployment (see
# docs/deployment/STAGING-AWS-AND-SOLANA-DEVNET.md Part G). Replace
# staging.example.com with the real hostname before use.
#
# What this does and does not protect against: this is a reverse proxy, not
# a WAF. A pentest against a deployment running an earlier version of this
# file found SQL-injection payloads, known-bad-input probes and a
# 200-request flood all reaching the origin application unfiltered -- the
# admin/metrics block below was the only filtering happening at this edge
# at all. infrastructure/terraform/waf.tf defines real AWS WAF rules for
# exactly this (rate limiting, managed SQLi/known-bad-input rule sets, IP
# reputation), but they only protect a deployment that sits behind an AWS
# ALB; this single-VM Caddy deployment does not, and cannot attach to them.
#
# Real options for this deployment shape, in order of effort:
#   1. Put a proxying CDN in front (e.g. Cloudflare, proxied DNS, not just
#      a tunnel): its free tier includes basic DDoS mitigation and request
#      rate limiting at the edge, in front of this box entirely.
#   2. Build Caddy with github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit (via xcaddy) to
#      get the rate_limit directive below working -- the stock
#      `apt install caddy` package this doc uses does NOT include it, so
#      the directive is commented out rather than present and silently
#      inert.
#   3. Move this workload behind a real AWS ALB so waf.tf's Web ACLs (rate
#      limiting + managed rule sets) can actually attach via
#      aws_wafv2_web_acl_association (var.alb_arn in variables.tf).

staging.example.com {
    @internal path /admin* /metrics*
    respond @internal 404

    # Requires a Caddy build with github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit (xcaddy
    # build --with github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit). Uncomment once that
    # binary is installed; left inactive here because the directive is a
    # config error on a plugin-less Caddy build.
    # rate_limit {
    #     zone dynamic {
    #         key    {remote_host}
    #         events 300
    #         window 1m
    #     }
    # }

    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000
}
